<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/foundational/how-to-read-a-story">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Stories are tension machines. They open a question (<em>will she escape? what is he
    hiding?</em>), raise the stakes, spend the reader's patience wisely, and pay it off. The
    German novelist Gustav Freytag drew this shape in 1863 — and once you have seen it, you will
    notice it hiding under nearly every film, novel and TV season you love, even the ones that
    set out to break it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the dramatic arc — exposition, rising action, climax (the peak), falling action, denouement" widget="plotter" config={freytagCfg} />

  <p>
    Hover across the curve: the flat start is <strong>exposition</strong> (who, where, what's
    normal); the climb is <strong>rising action</strong> (complications stack); the peak is the
    <strong>climax</strong> (the question answers itself); the descent is <strong>falling
    action</strong>, and the flattening end is the <strong>denouement</strong> — French for
    "untying".
  </p>

  <h2>Reading below the plot</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Character is choice under pressure.</strong> Watch what a character <em>does</em> when the stakes peak — that, not the description, is who they are.</li>
    <li><strong>Voice is a point of view with a style.</strong> Who tells the story decides what can be known — and what is being hidden from you.</li>
    <li><strong>Setting is never furniture.</strong> Where and when a story happens is an argument about how the world works.</li>
    <li><strong>Theme is the question a story keeps asking</strong> — usually answered ambiguously on purpose. If a story tells you its theme outright, it's an essay wearing a costume.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Breaking the arc on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    Modern literature loves the deliberate violation: starting <em>in medias res</em> (mid-climb),
    telling events out of order, or refusing the peak entirely (Kafka's trials never resolve).
    The violations work <em>because</em> you know the shape — avant-garde is a promise made
    against a convention. First learn the arc; then earn the detour.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Freytag, Die Technik des Dramas (1863); Forster, Aspects of the Novel (1927); standard narratology (Genette).</p>
</TopicPage>
